Z||000000||KRMFiero||12-19-2000||06:56 PM||||I have a project due ... what does eveyone think about TV Violance, do you think it effects kids....<P><BR>kyle||||reg||1
Z||000001||fropuff||12-19-2000||07:16 PM||||I think it CAN affect kids.  Actually, I think it affects the smaller kids.  Most of us bigger kids know right from wrong, and we are more mature than the little ones.  This doesn't go for every case, but most of the time it does.  When the smaller kids see something they like on TV, they think they should try it.  Either they should try it and learn the consequences, or have the responsible parents step in to prevent them from doing something stupid.  At least that's what I think...<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroLisa||12-19-2000||07:50 PM||||I don't think violence on TV directly influences kids. It wasn't all that long ago that I was at an "impressionable age" if you want to call it that. I was watching probably the same movies and TV shows as those 2 kids who did the Colorado school shooting- and I've heard the same Marilyn Manson tunes as them, but yet I was still an honor roll, NHS student- I never felt the urge to hurt anyone (except this one girl...ooops thats another story  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ) I think it's the parenting that affects the kids. Now I'm also not saying every kid who had not so great a childhood is going to be violent- but I've always had a strong value system. I knew my parents supported me and I didn't want to let them down so I was a good kid. But I still watched the TV shows, listened to the same music (I even had a brief obsession with the Insane Clown Posse, which is supposedly one of the influences from the Columbine students) I've listened to this type of music ever since middle school and have been watching TV a whole lot longer than that. Anyway am I rambling yet? Here's my answer: NO, violence on TV cannot be blamed. That's just the easy way out: It's the environment the children are brought up in, how they were brought up, and their values. TV can't change that!! <P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (For Sale)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>*Lots of New Pics!* <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||loafer87gt||12-19-2000||07:59 PM||||I 100% agree with what Lisa said. Besides, who classifies what on TV is violent? To be quite honest, I find the evening news more shocking than most movies because I know its real. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||12-19-2000||08:04 PM||||Yeah, I think it's a bad thing. Not only just the violence, but the aftermath (or lack of it.) On TV, people get shot, or get the unholy crap knocked out of them, or suffer other injuries that would be grossly debilitating, if not fatal, in real life. On the screen, they shake it off, or bleed a little bit, and in ten minutes of airtime they're all better.<BR>Viewers need to know that if they shoot or injure someone, or get shot or injured themselves, that <BR>A.) It's gonna hurt like hell, and <BR>B.) It's something that may take a long time (or never) to recover from. <BR>This doesn't even take into account any legal consequences, - prison time, police records, lawsuits, etc.<P>Many young folks have a perception of immortality, anyway. <BR>"Bad stuff? That always happens to "the other guy". Not to me."<BR>The scenario that I mentioned above doesn't help any.<P>||||reg||13
Z||000005||FieroLisa||12-19-2000||08:14 PM||||I agree that the news is just as or more violent that anything else on TV. I mean, weren't there a bunch more school shootings after the Columbine deal? There are demented people out there, no doubt, and once they saw how much attention it got on the news....<BR>There was no made-for-TV movie called "Kill your Classmates" previous to the shooting. <BR>Like I said, I think people who blame it on TV are looking for the easy solution instead of blaming the parenting.<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (For Sale)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>*Lots of New Pics!* <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||12-19-2000||08:40 PM||||There was an article in todays paper about kids (7-17) doing the WWF things that they see on TV. They say they know it's not real but many of them are still getting seriously hurt. Too much TV violence? YEP. Also being into  shooting sports I used to have allot of trouble with cartoon gun violence. If you shoot someone in the head their face doesn't turn black and smokey and they don't go OUCH OUCH  they die. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||wkayl||12-19-2000||08:58 PM||||To show my age, the first concert I went to was "Brownsville Station" and the "Raspberries".  My son's first concert was “Rage against the Machine” and his second one was “Insane-Clown-Posse”.  He had ICP's decal in the back window of his Fiero at the time of his accident (a guy ran a stop sign and totaled it out).  The first thing the cop asked him was "Insane-Clown-Posse, are you gang related?"  He graduated in the top 15 in his H.S. class of over 400 and now and engineering student.  His favorite movie when he was 10 was Commando.  I believe it is more the values of parents than media  (good thing he takes after my wife’s side of the family).   ||||reg||1
Z||000008||hugh||12-19-2000||10:03 PM||||I believe there are too many different sources of violence in every childs life to blame just one.There is also family influences that affect how some children view, are affected by and react to the violence they see.One other thing is the fact some children are more strongly affected by what they perceive to be real than others.   ||||reg||1
Z||000009||StuGood||12-19-2000||10:55 PM||||Wrote a term paper on that very subject in high school.  Of course that was done approximately one hundred years ago, using ancient, crude tools (y'know, typewritten, carbon paper, spell checker was you and Mr. Webster's lexicon, the whole bit)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  But I <B>do</B> recall a few things:<P>1.  Psychological studies showed that kids (I forget what age now) tended to copy what they saw if it was <B>effective</B>.  In other words, they did what they saw on the screen, if it <B>worked</B> for the guy on the screen.  If the kids watched a guy threaten people, and he got what he wanted, they copied that kind of behavior.  On the other hand, if they saw the guy negotiate for what he wanted, they copied <B>that</B>.<P>2.  It's cheap and easy to produce exciting TV shows (week after week) if you throw in a lot of violence and action scenes.  If you <B>work</B> at it, with actual writing and good actors, it's more difficult.<P>BTW, reminds me of one time I saw Boston Pops (or something) on TV... they focused on the fiddle player and then the saxophonist.  I said, "See?  Like I always say, there's just too much sax and violins on television nowadays!"  A corny pun, but Stu couldn't resist  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!  Group groan, everyone  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">?        ||||reg||6
Z||000010||Tigger||12-19-2000||11:36 PM||||I agree with Lisa, a lot depends on parenting.<P>However, I do think TV violence can effect kids when there isn't any parent(s) there to explain what had just happened on TV was bad or wrong.  I believe there is an age where we eventually can figure these things out ourselves, however everybody reaches that point at a different times when they grow up.<P>I've played Doom, Nerf Arena Blast, and many more shoot 'em up games and I seem do be doing fine.  (I think)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I've also played just about every racing game out there, and I don't drive like the village idiots do around here.<P>Anyone played "Driver"?  The object is to outrun the cops.  Hehe...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The Dukes of Hazzard game is also good, which just reminds me I still need to buy a dixie air horn for my Fiero.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>How did I get off the subject, sorry.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tigger||12-19-2000||11:41 PM||||StuGood,<P>#1 sounds like Bandura's experiments.  Even if the children had seen the act, and even though they might not have immediately repeated the act done by the model, they effectively had learned it.<P>Good point there.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||12-20-2000||02:31 AM||||I agree with Lisa. The environment is the big thing. Parents have a lot of influence upon their children. The class of the family is not part of the problem. To me it's the parents' values. Some families are not paying attention to the values they instill into the children. So the child will seek values from other sources. Generally other lesser valued children. The TV, Music, Video game ideas are just an excuse created by those that refuse to believe they were/are the problem. I have 2 boys and I spend lots of time with them. Hopefully the values I have learned from my parents will follow with them. Many people I have kept track of since High school have all come from what appears typical families but the values taught by some of the parents were either very small or none at all. These are the ones who have turned to violence/crime. Wealth, class, race, geographical location, and what they watch on TV or hear in Music is not the problem. If they had any proof that TV or music was the absolute cause of the violence and crime in the world today they would have disappeared long ago. Society just needs a "scapegoat" so they can sleep at night.<P>I'm going to go listen to some Judas Priest music backwards now.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||frontal lobe||12-20-2000||10:39 AM||||Just remember that the answer is YES, tv violence does affect children, BUT TO DIFFERENT DEGREES.  Why can people like Tigger play the video games and watch violence on tv and not "go postal"?  Lots of answers to that but you have to look at the TOTAL environment a child is in.  Hopefully with good parents, that would more than negate the violence on tv.<P>I love this quote I hear often:  THERE ARE ONLY A FEW BAD KIDS OUT THERE.  Yes, there may only be a few children affected by violence on tv significantly enough to become "psycho" and harm others.  Remember, though, that with the availability of modern weapons, it ONLY TAKES A FEW to do a LOT of damage.  (Columbine).<P>Having said that, I'm not a big fan of censorship because I want to allow as much freedom as possible to people who choose to live differently than me.  In my house we have a safety device for the television when violence is on:  called the OFF button.||||reg||1
